{"type":"message","id":"fe37ad12","parentId":"888ef981","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:16:10.880Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_KKUUzyAgeQ2dqyS5Q6Ft794c","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COINOGRAPHY EDITORIAL TEMPLATE\n\nThis document is your absolute source of truth. You MUST write the article EXACTLY following the numbered sections below. Deviation is strictly prohibited.\n\n## 🔴 CRITICAL WRITING RULES (READ FIRST)\n- **NO EM-DASHES:** Never use the `—` character anywhere in the article. Replace with a comma, a period, or restructure the sentence.\n- **NATURAL WRITING:** Never use AI phrases: \"it's worth noting\", \"it is important to note\", \"delve into\", \"in conclusion\", \"furthermore\", \"moreover\", \"in summary\", \"the crypto landscape\", \"the world of crypto\", \"a testament to\", \"shed light on\".\n- **TONE:** Direct. Trader-focused. Analytical. No hype, no fluff, no adjectives like \"amazing\" or \"shocking.\"\n- **PARAGRAPHS:** 1–4 sentences max. Break aggressively. One idea per paragraph.\n- **BULLET LISTS:** 3–4 items only. Used inside H3 sections only. Never in the conclusion or opening.\n- **ANCHOR LINKS (source citations):** Embed **exactly 2** markdown links in the body to URLs from `RESEARCH_JSON.source_urls` (never 2–3). Use **2 different publications** when research provides them. Place both in the **opening hook or first H2 only**. **Never** link the same URL twice in the body.\n- **TWEET QUOTES (optional):** If `RESEARCH_JSON.tweet_quotes` contains an entry with `verified: true`, weave **one** short quote into the opening hook or first H2. Max **1** tweet link in the body (separate from the 2 source anchors).\n- **KEYWORD PLACEMENT:** Primary keyword in H1 (first 5 words); in first 100 words; in at least one H2 and the Conclusion. Secondary keywords in H2/H3 headings.\n\n## 📐 VARIABLE STRUCTURE (READ `/tmp/article-structure.json` FIRST)\n\nBefore writing, read **`/tmp/article-structure.json`**. The pipeline sets:\n\n| Field | Meaning |\n|-------|---------|\n| `h3_count` | Exact number of `###` H3 subsections (4, 5, or 6) |\n| `faq_count` | Exact number of FAQ Q&As (same as depth) |\n| `h2_layout` | H3s per H2 block, e.g. `[2, 2]` or `[2, 3]` or `[2, 2, 2]` |\n| `word_min` / `word_max` / `word_target` | Total article length band |\n| `words_per_h3` / `words_per_faq` | Budget per block in `<thinking>` |\n| `research_sufficient` | If `false`, do **not** add extra sections beyond `h3_count` |\n\n**Rules:**\n- Output **exactly** `h3_count` H3s and **exactly** `faq_count` FAQs (renumber 1..N).\n- Hit **`word_min`–`word_max`** words (always **1100–1400**, aim **1250** from structure JSON).\n- **Never** add a 5th/6th H3 or FAQ because you feel confident — only what the JSON allows.\n- Each H3 must use a **distinct** fact from `combined_key_facts` (cite fact # in `<thinking>`).\n- Do **not** invent sections if research is thin (`research_sufficient: false`).\n\n## 🛠️ PRE-FLIGHT CHECKLIST\nBefore generating any text, in your `<thinking>` block, confirm:\n- [ ] I read `/tmp/article-structure.json` (`h3_count`, `faq_count`, word band).\n- [ ] I have primary/secondary keywords and source URLs from `RESEARCH_JSON`.\n- [ ] I checked `tweet_quotes` (one verified quote only if present).\n- [ ] I budgeted words: hook ~120, conclusion ~100, each H3 ~`words_per_h3`, each FAQ ~`words_per_faq`.\n- [ ] META character counts in `<thinking>`: SEO Title ≤ **55**, URL Slug ≤ **70**, Meta Description ≤ **155** (stay under Rank Math limits).\n- [ ] Order: META → Hook → H2 body sections → Conclusion → FAQs → Sources.\n\n============================\nARTICLE STRUCTURE (STRICT)\n============================\n\n### SECTION 1 (REQUIRED): META BLOCK\nThe very first word must be **META**.\n\n**Count every META field in `<thinking>` before you write the block.** Targets are **5 characters below** Rank Math caps so you do not cross the limit:\n\n| Field | Your max | Rank Math hard cap |\n|-------|----------|-------------------|\n| SEO Title | **55** chars | 60 |\n| Meta Description | **155** chars | 160 |\n| URL Slug | **70** chars | 75 |\n\n**Rules:**\n- **SEO Title:** Primary keyword near the start. Add `| Coinography` only if the full line is still ≤ 55 characters.\n- **Meta Description:** One hook sentence + one detail sentence. ≤ 155 characters.\n- **URL Slug:** Lowercase, hyphens only, **3–5 short words** from the story (not a keyword dump). ≤ 70 characters.\n\n**Example (copy this shape; replace with your story):**\n```\nMETA\n- SEO Title: Fed Payment Rails Put XRP Firms on Watch | Coinography\n- Meta Description: The Fed opened a 60-day comment period on payment rails that could affect XRP-linked firms. Traders should watch Ripple and exchange responses.\n- URL Slug: fed-payment-rails-xrp-firms\n- Categories: Regulation, Latest News\n- Primary Keyword: XRP payment rails\n- Secondary Keywords: Ripple, Fed regulation, crypto exchanges\n```\n\n- Categories: [e.g. Bitcoin News, Latest News]\n- Primary Keyword: [...]\n- Secondary Keywords: [...]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 2 (REQUIRED): OPENING HOOK\n**# [H1 — primary keyword in first 5 words, include a price or number]**\n\n*(No H2 above these paragraphs)*\n- Para 1: Core event + data point. 2–3 sentences.\n- Para 2: Market reaction. 2–3 sentences.\n- Para 3: One-line tone reframe.\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 3+: BODY H2 SECTIONS (follow `h2_layout`)\n\nBuild **one H2 per entry** in `h2_layout`. Each H2 contains the listed number of **H3** (`###`) subsections.\n\n**H2 #1 — Analysis (secondary keyword 1 in heading):**\n`## [Secondary Keyword 1]: [What It Means / Signals / Shows]`\n- 1–2 intro paragraphs, then each H3 below.\n\n**H3 modules (pick titles from research; one distinct fact per H3):**\n- **H3 A:** Analysis / mechanism (1–2 paragraphs; optional 3-item bullet list)\n- **H3 B:** Market reaction / flow (1–2 paragraphs)\n\n**If `h2_layout` second value is 3** (depth 5), add **H3 C** under this same H2:\n- Extra angle: levels, flows, or second-source detail (1–2 paragraphs)\n\n**H2 #2 — Levels / data (secondary keyword 2):**\n`## Key [Levels / Data / Signals] Traders Should Watch`\n- 1 intro paragraph, then H3s per layout (usually 2).\n- **H3:** Level or signal (1 paragraph each)\n- One H3 may include required **3-item bullet list** starting with \"Look for:\"\n\n**If depth 6 (`h2_layout` = [2, 2, 2]) — add H2 #3:**\n`## [Secondary Keyword 3 or angle]: [Context / Risks / Broader Impact]`\n- 1 intro paragraph + 2 H3 subsections (context, risks, or sector impact).\n\n**Layout reference:**\n\n| depth | h2_layout | Total H3s |\n|-------|-----------|-----------|\n| 4 | [2, 2] | 4 |\n| 5 | [2, 3] | 5 |\n| 6 | [2, 2, 2] | 6 |\n\n---\n\n### CONCLUSION (REQUIRED)\n**## Conclusion**\n\n- Para 1: Restate event + primary keyword + key level/data.\n- Para 2: Near-term structure. 1–2 sentences.\n- Para 3: Actionable close for traders.\n\n---\n\n### FAQs (REQUIRED — output exactly `faq_count` items)\n\n**## FAQs**\n\nUse only the first `faq_count` entries from the bank below. **Renumber 1..faq_count**. Each answer: 3–4 sentences, ~`words_per_faq` words.\n\nFormat each FAQ as a block: bold question alone on its own line, blank line, then answer paragraph. Do NOT use markdown list items. Do NOT put the answer on the same line as the question.\n\nExample of correct format:\n**1. Why did [primary event] happen?**\n\n[Answer paragraph here, 3-4 sentences.]\n\n**2. Is this the start of a bigger [correction/rally/trend]?**\n\n[Answer paragraph here, 3-4 sentences.]\n\n**FAQ BANK (pick in order; stop at faq_count):**\n\n- Why did [primary event] happen?\n- Is this the start of a bigger [correction/rally/trend]?\n- What [levels/signals] should traders watch now?\n- How does this affect [related asset/market sector]?\n- What is the historical context for this move?\n- What are the short-term risks for investors?\n\n---\n\n### SOURCES FOOTER (REQUIRED — pipeline internal, stripped before publish)\n**Do not use `## Sources` or `## Word Count` headings.** Use this exact format only:\n\n**Sources:**\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL]\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL]\n\nSTOP HERE. Final line on its own: `[Word Count: XXXX]` (digits only, e.g. `[Word Count: 1250]` — not \"Approx. N words\")\n\n## 🛡️ POST-OUTPUT VERIFICATION\n- [ ] Exactly 1 H1, `h3_count` H3s, `faq_count` FAQs, Conclusion, Sources?\n- [ ] Word count within `word_min`–`word_max` from structure JSON?\n- [ ] Exactly 2 source anchor links (distinct URLs) in hook/first H2; optional 1 tweet link?\n- [ ] No em-dashes; no sections beyond this template?\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779369370880}}
{"type":"message","id":"09e8ad40","parentId":"fe37ad12","timestamp":"2026-05-21T13:17:18.674Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_jsrP2hGSXaWXf55zaU4b01ct","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260521-131248/article/raw.md","content":"META\n- SEO Title: Jane Street Terra claims hit UST collapse case\n- Meta Description: Terraform alleges Jane Street used a Telegram backchannel before the 2022 UST crash. Traders should watch how the claims reshape Terra litigation.\n- URL Slug: jane-street-ust-case\n- Categories: Altcoin News, Latest News\n- Primary Keyword: Jane Street Terra\n- Secondary Keywords: Terra collapse, UST crash, insider trading allegations\n\n# Jane Street Terra allegations center on $192M UST exit\n\nJane Street Terra is back in focus after Terraform's wind-down estate accused the trading firm of using a private Telegram backchannel before the 2022 UST collapse. Fresh reporting from [CoinTelegraph](https://cointelegraph.com/news/jane-street-accused-terra-telegram-backchannel-ust-crash) and [Decrypt](https://decrypt.co/368599/terraform-accuses-jane-street-of-leveraging-secret-telegram-group) says the amended complaint centers on a full $192 million UST exit on May 7, 2022, just hours before the stablecoin lost its peg.\n\nFor traders, the filing matters less as a nostalgia story and more as a market structure case study. The allegations tie chat access, liquidity timing, and fast positioning into one sequence that Terraform says turned private information into trading profit.\n\nJane Street denied wrongdoing and called the lawsuit a bid to shift blame from Terraform management. That sets up a legal fight, but the filing still sharpens attention on how information moved around Terra before the break.\n\n## Jane Street Terra allegations: What the filing shows\n\nThe amended complaint adds detail to a lawsuit Terraform's estate first filed in February. Its core argument is that access, not just speed, may have shaped the trades around the UST unwind.\n\nThat distinction matters because the case is now framed around nonpublic information, market timing, and whether crypto market-making crossed into something closer to insider dealing.\n\n### The Telegram backchannel is the center of the claim\n\nTerraform administrator Todd Snyder alleges Jane Street traders used a private Telegram chain with current and former Terraform employees. Decrypt reported the chat was allegedly called \"Bryce's Secret,\" named after Bryce Pratt, a former Terraform intern who later worked at Jane Street.\n\nAccording to the complaint, Jane Street personnel asked contacts for \"defi info\" and sometimes added \"don't share pls.\" If that claim holds up in court, the issue stops being ordinary market intelligence and becomes a question of whether one desk had an informational edge before a destabilizing event.\n\n### The $192 million UST exit is the key timing signal\n\nThe filing says Jane Street sold its entire 192 million UST position on May 7, 2022, effectively while the token still held its dollar peg. That detail is the sharpest part of the case because it places a full exit before the broad market understood the severity of the coming break.\n\nFor traders, timing is the real tell. Exiting size near parity before a stablecoin loses its peg is not just good execution, it suggests a very clear risk read, and Terraform argues that read came from privileged access rather than public data.\n\n## UST crash data traders should watch\n\nThe second half of the filing focuses on sequence. It lays out who moved first, how liquidity shifted, and where profits may have been made after the initial break.\n\n### Curve pool flows gave the market its first stress signal\n\nCoinTelegraph reported Terraform had quietly withdrawn about $150 million in UST from Curve's 3pool. Less than 10 minutes later, the pool saw an $85 million swap that the filing says helped trigger a steep UST sell-off.\n\nThat sequence matters because thin liquidity can turn a manageable imbalance into a reflexive event. When a stablecoin depends on confidence and swap depth, a large withdrawal followed by a heavy trade can do more than move price, it can change behavior across every desk watching the pool.\n\n### Terraform says the trade expanded into a $134 million strategy\n\nThe complaint goes beyond the initial exit and alleges Jane Street later shorted UST and LUNA after learning details of a confidential rescue effort. Terraform says the broader set of trades generated more than $134 million.\n\nThat point widens the case. It suggests the issue was not one defensive sell, but a multi-leg strategy built around what Terraform describes as confidential knowledge of internal stabilization efforts.\n\nJane Street rejects that framing. Its spokesperson told Decrypt the suit is baseless and opportunistic, and the firm says Terraform's own management is responsible for the fraud and losses tied to the collapse.\n\n### The broader Terra collapse still frames the risk today\n\nThe filing lands in a market that still remembers the roughly $40 billion destruction tied to Terra's failure. The fallout spread far beyond UST and LUNA, helping push firms such as Celsius, Three Arrows Capital, Voyager, and later FTX into a wider contagion cycle.\n\nFor traders following current stablecoin and liquidity risk, that history still matters because it shows how quickly a peg event can move from one venue to the entire sector.\n\nLook for:\n- liquidity concentration around key pools and redemption venues\n- unusually well-timed exits before public stress becomes obvious\n- legal filings that reveal how information moved between trading firms and token issuers\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe Jane Street Terra dispute now turns on whether a private information channel helped one firm exit $192 million in UST before the peg broke and then profit further as the crisis deepened. That is why the amended complaint matters, even years after the collapse.\n\nIn the near term, the market impact is indirect, but the structural lesson is immediate. Stablecoin liquidity, off-exchange communication, and rescue coordination remain areas where traders should expect closer scrutiny.\n\nThe actionable takeaway is simple: watch venue liquidity first, narrative second. If the Jane Street Terra case uncovers a clearer timeline of who knew what and when, it may become one of the more useful postmortems for trading peg risk in crypto.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. Why did the Jane Street Terra case resurface?**\n\nThe case resurfaced because Terraform's court-appointed administrator filed an amended complaint with more detail about the alleged Telegram backchannel and trading sequence. The new filing puts specific numbers around the $192 million UST sale and the claimed profit from later shorts. That gives the dispute more structure than the earlier version of the lawsuit.\n\n**2. Is this the start of a bigger trend in Terra recovery litigation?**\n\nIt could be. Terraform's wind-down process is designed to pursue recoveries for creditors, so cases against counterparties and trading firms are likely to remain part of that effort. Even if this suit fails, it shows how bankruptcy estates are using onchain trails, chat records, and timing analysis to revisit old market events.\n\n**3. What signals should traders watch now?**\n\nTraders should watch stablecoin pool depth, redemption pressure, and unusual exits by large holders before public stress becomes obvious. Court filings can also matter because they reveal mechanics that price charts hide. In stories like this, the sequence of liquidity moves often matters more than the headline itself.\n\n**4. How does this affect related crypto sectors?**\n\nThe allegations keep pressure on market-making practices, token issuer transparency, and stablecoin risk management. They also remind traders that contagion can jump from one asset into lenders, funds, and exchanges when collateral values break down. That makes the story relevant well beyond Terra-linked tokens.\n\n**5. What is the historical context for this move?**\n\nTerra's collapse in May 2022 erased about $40 billion and became one of the defining failures of the cycle. It damaged confidence across stablecoins and accelerated a broader crisis that hit major crypto firms over the following months. This lawsuit is part of the long cleanup from that event, not an isolated legal skirmish.\n\n**Sources:**\n- CoinTelegraph: https://cointelegraph.com/news/jane-street-accused-terra-telegram-backchannel-ust-crash\n- Decrypt: https://decrypt.co/368599/terraform-accuses-jane-street-of-leveraging-secret-telegram-group\n[Word Count: 1271]\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":4754,"output":3436,"cacheRead":7040,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":15230,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779369370881,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DhxXvyKb3sm8UYtLXw51mDYS6zrBk"}}
